# TYPES ARE DATA — and so are declarations.
#
# `examples/code.braid` reflects a PROGRAM: `reflect` turns a quotation
# into its spine and the list library becomes the metaprogramming
# library.  This file reflects the other half — the program's TYPE, and
# the DECLARATION it was written against.
#
#     typeOfWord : Str     ⇒ (TypeRep | Str)     a word's principal scheme
#     typeOfCode : Code    ⇒ (TypeRep | Str)     a Code value's, inferred here
#     declOf     : Str     ⇒ (Decl | Str)        a data/type/theory declaration
#     showType   : TypeRep ⇒ Str                 the display `:t` prints
#     envOf      : • ⇒ List(Box(Str TypeRep))    every word in scope
#
# Nothing here computes anything new.  Every one of these words answers
# with a fact the checker already holds, so they are pure, they cost
# nothing, and a `functor` may call them — which is what makes an
# ELABORATION-TIME derivation an ordinary Braid word rather than a
# construct of its own (§5 below).
#
# THE ONE RULE ABOUT EQUALITY.  These words NORMALIZE before they
# answer (`normalizeArrow`: variables become a0, ρ0, σ0, ε0 in order of
# first appearance), so two schemes are the same scheme exactly when
# their reps are `eq?`.  A rep you assembled by hand carries no such
# guarantee — `eq?` on an unnormalized rep compares variable NAMES,
# which is a different question.  MANUAL §12.

def say = ((t -> t ; showType) | (e -> e)) ; merge

"-- a word's scheme, as data, rendered back --" ; print
("dup  : " ("dup" ; typeOfWord ; say) ; cat) ; print       # a0 ⇒ a0 a0
("fold : " ("fold" ; typeOfWord ; say) ; cat) ; print
("and  : " ("and" ; typeOfWord ; say) ; cat) ; print       # Bool Bool ⇒ Bool
#   `and`'s rep is a structural sum; the display folds it back to
#   `Bool`, because the display is the REPL's own — one renderer for a
#   type, reached two ways.

"" ; print
"-- and what has no rep, with the reason --" ; print
("pack : " ("pack" ; typeOfWord ; say) ; cat) ; print
#   a bundle exponent is a WIDTH, not a type.  A rep that flattened it
#   would make two different types equal, which is the one thing a rep
#   must never do — so it rides the miss track and `:t pack` still
#   prints.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. DIAGRAM CUTS — what runs beside what
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# A spine is a string diagram.  Its ATOMS are nodes and its WIRES are
# edges, so "which parts of this program are independent of which" is a
# question about connected components — and the arities that say which
# wire an atom eats come from `typeOfCode`, one atom at a time.
#
# `cuts : Code ⇒ List(Code)` splits a spine into those components.  Two
# halves of a tensor with no wire between them come back separately;
# what remains between components is the dependency structure, which is
# to say none.
#
# WHAT THIS IS AND IS NOT.  It is ANALYSIS, not runtime parallelism —
# nothing here runs anything anywhere.  What it licenses is a reading:
# a component whose grade is ∅ is CENTRAL (interchange holds for it),
# so it may be reordered against anything and run wherever it likes; a
# component carrying a label may not, because the label is exactly the
# claim that its order is observable.  The grade is on the arrow, so
# `typeOfCode` of a component answers that question too.

## the walk's state: the next fresh component id, the live stack's ids,
## the current stage's outputs and tags, and the stages already done
data Cut = (Int List(Int) List(Int) List(Int) List(List(Int)))

## MERGING is one relabelling, which is why every id-carrying field of
## the state is rewritten together
def relab    = (old new l -> l ; [(x -> (x old ; equals) new x ; select)] ... ; map)
def relabAll = (old new ls -> ls ; [(l -> old new l ; relab)] ... ; map)
def relabCut =
    old new c ->
    c ; unCut
      ; (n s o t d -> n (old new s ; relab) (old new o ; relab) (old new t ; relab) \
                        (old new d ; relabAll) ; Cut)

## an ATOM's arity, read off its own type: `typeOfCode` of the one-atom
## spine, and the closed width of each side of the arrow
def arrWidths =
    [(s -> 0 0 ; Box)] [(s -> 0 0 ; Box)] [(nm as -> 0 0 ; Box)] [(x -> 0 0 ; Box)] \
    [(as tl -> 0 0 ; Box)] [(s -> 0 0 ; Box)] \
    [(i o ls tl -> (i ; stackWidth) (o ; stackWidth) ; Box)] ... ; foldTypeRep

def atomArity =
    a -> ((a ; single) ; single) ; typeOfCode
       ; ((r -> r ; arrWidths) | drop ; (0 0 ; Box)) ; merge

def headOr   = (z l -> l ; unList ; (z | (x r -> x)) ; merge)
def copies   = (m g -> (m ; range) ; [(i -> g)] ... ; map)
def cutMerge = (c cs g -> cs ; [(acc x -> x g acc ; relabCut)] c ... ; fold)
def freshIf  = (es n -> (es ; len ; zero) (n 1 ; +) n ; select)
def groupIf  = (es n -> (es ; len ; zero) n (0 es ; headOr) ; select)

## one atom: eat k wires off the front, join their components, and push
## m copies of the joined id back.  An atom that eats nothing starts a
## component of its own.
def cutAtom =
    c a ->
    a ; atomArity ; unBox ; (k m ->
      c ; unCut ; (n s o t d ->
        (k s ; take) ; (eaten ->
          (eaten n ; freshIf) ; (n2 ->
            (eaten n ; groupIf) ; (g ->
              ((n2 (k s ; skip) o t d ; Cut) eaten g ; cutMerge)
                ; unCut
                ; (n3 s3 o3 t3 d3 ->
                    n3 s3 ((m g ; copies) o3 ; append) (g t3 ; cons) d3 ; Cut))))))

## one stage: its atoms left to right, then its outputs go in front of
## whatever the stage did not touch
def cutStage =
    c stage ->
    stage ; [cutAtom] c ... ; fold ; unCut
      ; (n s o t d -> n ((o ; reverse) s ; append) nil nil ((t ; reverse) d ; cons) ; Cut)

def cutWalk =
    code ->
    code ; [cutStage] (0 nil nil nil nil ; Cut) ... ; fold ; unCut
         ; (n s o t d -> d ; reverse)

def elemI     = (x col -> col ; [(seen y -> seen (x y ; equals) ; or)] false ... ; fold)
def distinctI =
    col ->
    col ; [(seen x -> (x seen ; elemI) seen (x seen ; cons) ; select)] nil ... ; fold ; reverse

def pickStage =
    g stage ids ->
    (ids stage ; zip)
      ; [(p -> p ; unBox ; (i a -> (i g ; equals) (a ; single) nil ; select))] ... ; flatMap

def cutComponent =
    g code tags ->
    (code tags ; zip) ; [(e -> e ; unBox ; (st ids -> g st ids ; pickStage))] ... ; map
      ; [(s -> (s ; len ; zero) nil (s ; single) ; select)] ... ; flatMap

def cuts =
    code ->
    code ; cutWalk
         ; (tags -> (tags ; concat ; distinctI) ; [(g -> g code tags ; cutComponent)] ... ; map)

"" ; print
"-- the components of a spine --" ; print
[1 2 3 4 ; + _ _ ; _ *] ; getCode
  ; (c ->
      ("whole: " (c ; unparse) ; cat) ; print
      (c ; cuts) ; [(k -> "  cut: " (k ; unparse) ; cat)] ... ; map ; printAll)
#   whole: 1 2 3 4 >> + _ _ >> _ *
#     cut: 1 2 >> + >> _
#     cut: 3 4 >> _ _ >> *
#
# Two components, and nothing in the TEXT said so: the `_`s in stage two
# and stage three are what carry each half past the other, and the cut
# is read off the wires rather than off the layout.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. A TYPE-LEVEL FUNCTION — as a word
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# `Cols` is the column store's object map: a pair of columns is a pair
# of column-stores, and anything else becomes a column of itself.
#
#     Cols(P(x, y)) = P(Cols x, Cols y)          Cols t = List(t)
#
# In a type system this is a TYPE FAMILY, and a type family is
# NON-INJECTIVE: from `Cols a ~ Cols b` you cannot conclude `a ~ b`, so
# unification can no longer solve for a variable underneath one, and
# principal types go with it.  Here it is an ORDINARY WORD on `TypeRep`
# values — it may branch, recurse, fail, print — and it never enters
# unification at all.  That is the whole rule (design-macros.md,
# 2026-09-16): a type-level function is a Braid word run at
# elaboration, producing a declaration or a program.

data P(a, b) = a b

def listOf = (t -> .List ((t ; single) ; single) ; alt3 ; TypeRep)

def colsOf with Recursive =
    t ->
    t ; unTypeRep
      ; ( (s -> t ; listOf) \
        | (s -> t ; listOf) \
        | (nm as -> (((nm ; symStr) "P" ; equals) ((as ; len) 2 ; equals) ; and) \
                      [nm (as ; [(st -> st ; [colsOf] ... ; map)] ... ; map) ; alt3 ; TypeRep] \
                      [t ; listOf] ... ; cond) \
        | (x -> t ; listOf) \
        | (as tl -> t ; listOf) \
        | (s -> t ; listOf) \
        | (i o ls tl -> t ; listOf) ) ; mergeTypeRep

## the OUTPUT side of an arrow rep, and its first wire — how a word's
## result type is got at without any type syntax in the term
def arrOut =
    [(s -> nil)] [(s -> nil)] [(nm as -> nil)] [(x -> nil)] \
    [(as tl -> nil)] [(s -> nil)] [(i o ls tl -> o)] ... ; foldTypeRep
def noRep   = .none ; alt1 ; TypeRep
def headRep = (l -> l ; unList ; (noRep | (x r -> x)) ; merge)
def resultOf =
    w -> w ; typeOfWord ; ((r -> r ; arrOut ; headRep) | drop ; noRep) ; merge

def rowP = (x y -> (x 1 ; +) (y "" ; cat) ; P)
def anInt = (x -> x 1 ; +)

"" ; print
"-- a non-injective type-level function, living in the language --" ; print
("rowP  : " (("rowP" ; resultOf) ; showType) ; cat) ; print
("Cols  : " (("rowP" ; resultOf) ; colsOf ; showType) ; cat) ; print
("anInt : " (("anInt" ; resultOf) ; showType) ; cat) ; print
("Cols  : " (("anInt" ; resultOf) ; colsOf ; showType) ; cat) ; print
#   rowP  : P(Int, Str)
#   Cols  : P(List(Int), List(Str))
#   anInt : Int
#   Cols  : List(Int)
#
# What is NOT here: a way to turn that answer back into a DECLARATION.
# `colsOf` computes the column store's object map and prints it; to
# make `data ColsP = …` out of it we would need a declaration form
# whose body is computed, and that is the same gap §5 hits.  Nothing
# about the function is missing; what is missing is a door.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. THE BOOTSTRAP SEED — a differential check
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# `envOf` is every word in scope with its scheme.  For each of them
# there are TWO ways to ask what its type is: look the word up
# (`typeOfWord`), or hand the checker the one-atom program that calls
# it (`typeOfCode`).  They should agree, and this is the first rung of
# a longer ladder:
#
#   1. `typeOfWord w` = `typeOfCode [w]`, over everything in scope
#   2. a Braid CHECKER for a fragment of Braid, written on `TypeRep`
#   3. the two run against each other over the corpus
#   4. self-application: the Braid checker checks itself
#   5. optionally, a switch — the host checker becomes one
#      implementation of a specification written in the language it
#      checks
#
# Only rung 1 is claimed.  It is the honest first one, and the rep was
# designed to carry the rest of the climb: structural, total except for
# the width tier, normalizing, and with `eq?` for equality.

def sameShown =
    e ->
    e ; unBox
      ; (nm rep ->
          nm ; parse
             ; ( (c -> c ; typeOfCode
                        ; ((r -> (rep ; showType) (r ; showType) ; equals) | drop ; false) \
                        ; merge) \
               | drop ; false ) ; merge)

def sameRep =
    e ->
    e ; unBox
      ; (nm rep ->
          nm ; parse
             ; ( (c -> c ; typeOfCode ; ((r -> rep r ; eq? ; verdict) | drop ; false) ; merge) \
               | drop ; false ) ; merge)

def countIf = (f l -> l ; [(n e -> f e ; ev ; (b -> b (n 1 ; +) n ; select))] 0 ... ; fold)
def nameOf  = (e -> e ; unBox ; (nm rep -> nm))
def failing = (f l -> l ; [(e -> f e ; ev ; (b -> b nil (e ; nameOf ; single) ; select))] ... ; map ; concat)

"" ; print
"-- the differential check, over every word in scope --" ; print
("words with a rep: " (envOf ; len ; toStr) ; cat) ; print
("agree as displayed: " ([sameShown] envOf ; countIf ; toStr) ; cat) ; print
("agree as reps: " ([sameRep] envOf ; countIf ; toStr) ; cat) ; print
"displayed disagreements: " ; print
[sameShown] envOf ; failing ; [(n -> "  " n ; cat)] ... ; map ; printAll
#   The one name that cannot agree is `#fix`, and it cannot for a good
#   reason: since 2026-09-14 no source can NAME it (`#` opens a
#   comment), so `parse "#fix"` has nothing to parse.  `with Recursive`
#   emits it and nothing else does.
#
# THE RESIDUE, stated plainly.  Every word agrees on what is DISPLAYED
# and only about two thirds agree as REPS, and the difference is the
# EFFECT TAIL: a prim's scheme is written with a closed pure row
# (`arrPure`), while an inferred scheme's row is open (a fresh ε that
# later constraints may fill).  The display hides effect tails on
# purpose, so it hides exactly this.  It is a real difference — "pure,
# full stop" is a stronger claim than "pure so far" — and the honest
# report is both numbers rather than a comparison rigged to agree.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. DERIVING AT ELABORATION — a functor that reads a declaration
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# A `functor` is handed the body's `Code` and may return anything.
# Since the reflection words are pure and read the PREFIX SCOPE, a
# functor may call them — so a def whose body is a SYM naming a
# declaration can elaborate into the printer that declaration implies.
# Nothing runs at run time: what the def contains after elaboration IS
# the derived program, checked by ordinary inference like any other.

data Row = (label: Str, n: Int)

def symOfAtom =
    [(s -> s)] [(i -> .none)] [(t -> .none)] [(s -> s)] \
    [(c -> .none)] [(cs b -> .none)] [(c -> .none)] ... ; foldAtom

def firstSym =
    c ->
    c ; unList
      ; ( .none \
        | (st r -> st ; unList ; (.none | (a r2 -> a ; symOfAtom)) ; merge) ) ; merge

def cellsOfCode =
    c -> (c ; firstSym ; symStr) ; declOf ; ((d -> d ; cellsFor) | drop ; nil) ; merge

functor Cells = cellsOfCode

def rowCells with Cells = .Row
#   rowCells : Row =Cells> List(Str) — the body is the DERIVED printer,
#   and the `=Cells>` on the arrow is the receipt that says so.

"" ; print
"-- a printer derived at elaboration --" ; print
("x" 7 ; Row) ; rowCells ; [(s -> "  " s ; cat)] ... ; map ; printAll
("rowCells : " ("rowCells" ; typeOfWord ; say) ; cat) ; print

# THE GAP, and it is stage 8's.  That receipt is not decoration: it is
# part of the type, so `rowCells` no longer fits an `Fn⟨a ⇒ b⟩` — which
# is why `examples/frame.braid` derives `tradeCells` through a runtime
# `evalAs` against a witness instead of through a functor.  The receipt
# is right (a functor that leaves no receipt cannot be audited) and the
# refusal is right (a label is part of the type).  What is missing is a
# DECLARATION FORM WHOSE BODY IS COMPUTED — a way to declare a def from
# Code without applying a scope to it.  Until it exists, a derivation
# either pays a runtime `evalAs` or wears a label.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. WHAT IS NOT ADMITTED, on its own merits
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# There is no `∀a. a ⇒ TypeRep`.  Every word above takes a NAME or
# CODE — both static, both settled before the program runs — and none
# of them takes a WIRE.  That is what keeps the free theorems: `∀a.
# a ⇒ a` is still the identity, because nothing inside it can ask what
# `a` is.  Such a word is not refused; it is UNWRITABLE, because no
# atom produces a `TypeRep` from a value.
#
# And rewriting triggered by the manifest of the def BEING elaborated
# is impossible by construction: elaboration precedes inference, so
# when `Cells` ran above, `rowCells` had no type yet.  What a functor
# can read is the prefix scope — `Row`, `dup`, `cellsFor` — which was
# settled before this declaration was reached.  That is what "prefix
# scope" means, and it is the same property that lets a functor be RUN
# while the module is still being checked.
